The second degree equation will represent a pair of perpendicular straight lines, if
$\left|\begin{array}{lll}a & h & g \\ h & b & f \\ g & f & c\end{array}\right|=0$ and $a+b=0$
Given pair of line is
$12 x^2+7 x y-p y^2-18 x+q y+6=0\therefore \quad\left|\begin{array}{ccc}12 & 7 / 2 & -9 \\ 7 / 2 & -p & q / 2 \\ -9 & q / 2 & 6\end{array}\right|=0\begin{array}{ll}\text { and } & 12-p=0 \\ \Rightarrow & p=12\end{array}
$\therefore \quad\left|\begin{array}{ccc}12 & 7 / 2 & -9 \\ 7 / 2 & -12 & q / 2 \\ -9 & q / 2 & 6\end{array}\right|=0\begin{aligned} \Rightarrow \quad & 12\left(-72+\frac{q^2}{4}\right)-\frac{7}{2}\left(-21+\frac{9 q}{2}\right) \\ & -9\left(\frac{7 q}{4}-108\right)=0 \\ \Rightarrow \quad & -864-3 q^2-\frac{147}{2}-\frac{63 q}{4} \\ & -\frac{63 q}{4}+972=0 \Rightarrow q=1\end{aligned}
